   "mainEntity": [
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can a cold-climate heat pump heat a Chicago home when temperatures drop below zero?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Cold-climate heat pumps still pull usable heat from outdoor air well below freezing. Capacity drops as that air gets colder. So a Chicago system is planned around the coldest nights of the year, not an average winter day. City & Suburban installs American Standard heat pump equipment. It also sets the outdoor temperature at which a backup heat source takes over. That keeps the house warm through a deep cold snap. For most of a Chicago heating season the heat pump carries the load on its own, and the backup is there for the extremes."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How does City & Suburban decide whether a Chicago home suits a heat pump?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "The decision starts with the building, not the equipment. City & Suburban looks at how the home is heated now, whether usable ducts exist, and what the electrical service can carry. Placement counts as well. An outdoor unit needs clear space for airflow and for snow. Insulation and window area matter too, because a leaky bungalow needs more capacity on a design-day morning. A NATE-certified technician sees the house before anything is quoted. The answer can be a heat pump, a ductless system, or keeping the gas equipment for now."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Which Chicago buildings are a poor fit for heat pump heating, and what suits them better?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A heat pump is a weak fit where refrigerant lines have no route to run. It is weak again where no outdoor spot clears snow and allows airflow. It fits badly where the electrical panel has no spare capacity. A condo board may also bar equipment on the roof or in the gangway. Homes heated by steam or hot water may need boiler services. Without existing ducts, keeping the current hot-water or steam heat, or a ductless setup, usually fits better. City & Suburban says so when the building argues against a conversion, and will keep the existing home heating system running instead."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How is a heat pump sized for Chicago winter heating, and what happens when sizing is wrong?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Sizing is a heat-loss question before it is a product question. It covers floor area, insulation, window area, air leakage, and the coldest outdoor temperature the system has to meet. An oversized heat pump short cycles and swings room temperature. It can also leave rooms clammy in shoulder season. An undersized one leans on backup heat far more often than planned, which raises the running cost every winter. City & Suburban has a NATE-certified technician review the house and the existing ducts before a heat pump is quoted. Duct size caps airflow no matter how large the outdoor unit is."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What backup heat does a heat pump need through a Chicago winter?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Two arrangements are common. An all-electric heat pump carries resistance heat strips in the air handler. Those strips work in any weather, but draw heavy current on the coldest nights. The other route keeps an existing gas furnace in place to take over below a set outdoor temperature. That is the dual-fuel arrangement explained under hybrid heating. City & Suburban installs either arrangement with American Standard equipment. It sets the thermostat changeover so the backup fires only when the heat pump cannot keep up. Gas and electric rates at the address usually decide which route makes sense."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Will a heat pump cost more to run than a gas furnace in Chicago?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Not by itself. A heat pump moves heat rather than burning fuel, so it is efficient in most Chicago weather. The winter bill turns on how many hours the backup heat runs. Electric resistance backup is the costly mode, because it makes heat instead of moving it. So the changeover temperature, and the hours spent below it, decide the cost. A dual-fuel setup switches to gas at the point where gas is cheaper per unit of heat. City & Suburban sets the thermostat changeover on either arrangement."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What decides the price of heat pump installation for a Chicago home?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Capacity and efficiency tier come first. Then comes the work the building actually needs: electrical service changes, the route for refrigerant lines, a new air handler or coil, duct changes, and condensate drainage. Mounting the outdoor unit above drift level counts too, as do permit scope and the backup heat choice. As public context, heat pump permits filed with the City of Chicago in the 24 months to August 2026 carried a median declared value of $25,200. City & Suburban puts the exact price in writing before work begins, with no hidden fees. A proposal should separate heat pump equipment, installation work and backup heat requirements."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What happens during heat pump installation, and how long is the house without heat?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A straightforward changeover usually runs one to two days. It runs longer when electrical work or duct changes are part of the scope. Crews recover refrigerant from the old system, then set the new outdoor unit clear of snow drift. Line sets and control wiring follow. The indoor air handler or coil goes in, and the system is vacuumed, charged and tested. Chicago requires a permit for HVAC equipment replacement, as reflected in the City permit records. Equipment replacements in Chicago generally need a City permit, and the written quote should say who pulls it. The price is set in writing first, with no hidden fees, and the work carries a satisfaction guarantee. Andrew T. described the pace after an air conditioner replacement in July 2025: \"Time from first visit to installation was just a couple of days.\""
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What does a heat pump repair visit look like when the heat stops?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Diagnosis comes before parts. A technician reads what the thermostat is calling for, checks the control board and the reversing valve, and watches a defrost cycle. Refrigerant pressures, temperature split, and the outdoor fan and compressor circuits all get measured. Common failures include a contactor, a capacitor, a sensor, or a defrost control. City & Suburban quotes the repair in writing before starting it, with no hidden fees. It repairs all makes and models, not only American Standard. Parts that have to be ordered get a scheduled return rather than a guess."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "When does a heat pump failure in a Chicago winter count as an emergency?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "No heat in subfreezing weather is urgent. Pipes along exterior walls and in porch bathrooms can freeze within hours. Burning smells, smoke, repeated breaker trips, or a cabinet encased in ice are reasons to shut the system off at the disconnect. Rented housing also has a legal floor where the landlord supplies the heat. The heating season runs September 15 to June 1. The general rule requires at least 68 degrees from 8:30 a.m. to 10:30 p.m. It drops to 66 degrees overnight, under Chicago Municipal Code 13-196-410. City & Suburban runs emergency HVAC service every day till midnight, except Sundays, with same-day service when the schedule allows."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is a heat pump safer than a gas heating system, and does carbon monoxide still matter?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A heat pump moves heat with electricity and burns no fuel indoors, so the unit itself makes no combustion gas. That removes one hazard. It does not remove the alarm duty in a building that still runs other gas appliances. The Illinois Carbon Monoxide Alarm Detector Act (430 ILCS 135) is the governing rule for alarms near sleeping areas. City & Suburban installs American Standard heat pump equipment and repairs all makes and models. Electrical safety is the other half of the job: correct breaker sizing, correct wire, and a proper outdoor disconnect."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why does a heat pump frost over, and when does ice call for heat pump repair?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Light frost on the outdoor coil is normal in Chicago humidity. A healthy heat pump runs a defrost cycle that clears it and goes back to heating. A solid shell of ice points to a real fault, as does frost that rebuilds minutes after defrost. A cabinet standing in meltwater is another sign. The usual causes are a failed defrost board, a bad coil sensor, a stuck reversing valve, or low refrigerant charge. Shoveling snow back from the cabinet and keeping the unit above drift level prevents much of it. City & Suburban diagnoses the defrost circuit rather than topping off refrigerant, which hides a leak instead of finding it."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What separates City & Suburban from other Chicago heat pump contractors?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "City & Suburban has been family-owned since 1952. Rob, the second generation of the founding family, runs it today. That is why the same name turns up across repeat visits. Technicians are NATE-certified, pricing is upfront with no hidden fees, and the work carries a satisfaction guarantee. American Standard equipment is what gets installed, while repairs cover all makes and models. The company holds a 4.98 average across 204 Google reviews, 4.9 from 251 reviews on Yelp, and an A+ rating with the BBB. Coverage spans 21 service areas: 13 Chicago neighborhoods and 8 suburbs."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How is heat pump repair-or-replace advice handled, and what do homeowners report?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "City & Suburban prices the repair and the replacement before the homeowner has to choose. The reviews describe that pattern. Troy T. wrote in November 2025: \"They reviewed my options and helped me make the decision between repair and replace.\" The same choice came up on a failing furnace motor. Mardi K. wrote in February 2026: \"We did a bit of online searching and felt the pricing was fair.\" The same math applies to a tired heat pump. Repair cost is weighed against the years of service left, with both figures written down first."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Does a heat pump used for winter heating also handle summer cooling, and who maintains it?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "One heat pump both heats and cools. So a Chicago install commonly replaces the air conditioner in the same project. Summer performance and cooling repair belong with heat pump cooling service. Because the equipment works through both seasons, City & Suburban advises a check in the fall before the heating load arrives, and another in spring. Maintenance plans bundle those seasonal check-ups with priority dispatch and member discounts. Wells Fargo Home Projects financing is available on installations, subject to credit approval."
     }
    }
